// Falling Empire — interludes and outros.
// Variables set before running: $player_faction, $enemy_faction, $sector_name,
//   $mission_objective, $chapter_title, $date
// Outro also uses: $missions_won, $total_missions

title: falling_empire_begin
---
Narrator: {$player_faction} — Internal Record
Narrator: {$date}
Narrator: The sector is called {$sector_name}. 

A stable region. Worth defending.

Enemy movements from {$enemy_faction} trace themselves behind your eyes. even when you look away.
A system destabilizes. Another overcommits. You reinforce one front and create two new weaknesses.

You can see it happening. You are part of it happening.
Narrator: {$mission_objective}: defend the sector.

There is no version of this where you save everything. You know that. You've always known that.

Narrator: Date: {$date}. Official record marks this as an ongoing defensive action against {$enemy_faction} incursion.

===

title: falling_empire_mid
---
Narrator: {$player_faction} — Sealed Transmission
Narrator: {$date}
Narrator: The sector is called {$sector_name}. 

Me: I've been staring at the map too long.

Coordinates. Casualty numbers. Strategic assets reduced to past tense. You read them faster now. You have to. There's always another one waiting.

Captains asking for permission to hold position. Station commanders requesting reinforcements you don't have. Civilians routing questions through military channels because someone told them you would know what to do.

There will be reports. Summaries. After-action analyses. They will not capture this.
Narrator: There was a moment—early—when you thought you could push them back.

The latest transmission from {$enemy_faction} comes through filtered channels. Not meant for you, but not hidden either. They're already talking about what comes after. Like the outcome is decided.

The weight of the sector. Of {$sector_name}. Of every system that still lights up on your map because you haven't let it go yet.

===

title: falling_empire_end
---
Narrator: {$player_faction} — Final Command
Narrator: {$date}
Narrator: {$sector_name}. It's still there.

That's the first surprise. It shouldn't be.
Narrator: Final campaign status: {$player_faction} retains control over core systems within {$sector_name}. {$enemy_faction} offensive has been halted and structurally disrupted. Several outer systems remain lost or contested.

No one won cleanly. No one could.
You read evacuation logs, survival counts, fragmented reports from places that shouldn't still have people in them. And yet they do.

Each one feels like rebuilding something you don't fully remember designing. But you give them anyway. Because no one else can.
Narrator: Date: {$date}. Conflict concluded in partial outcome. {$sector_name} remains under {$player_faction} control with significant losses recorded.

You look at the map of {$sector_name}. It's scarred. But it's still there.

And for now— that's enough to begin again.
===

title: outro_falling_empire_win
---
Narrator: {$player_faction} — Final Command
Narrator: {$date}
Narrator: {$sector_name}. It's still there.

That's the first surprise. It shouldn't be.
Narrator: Final campaign status: {$player_faction} retains control over core systems within {$sector_name}. {$enemy_faction} offensive has been halted and structurally disrupted. Several outer systems remain lost or contested.

The war didn't end them. It wore them down to something that can no longer advance.

-> New Campaign
